# A short story for the Aside engine.
#
# TENTH REGISTER, AND THE FIRST WITH TWO NARRATORS. Every story before this has one voice. This one alternates:
# every scene is a letter, and the letters go back and forth between two people who ran a cinema together and are
# no longer in the same building. The reader sees both sides; neither of them does.
#
# That is the whole formal idea. The choices are what each of them WRITES - and because a letter cannot be
# unsent, a choice made in one scene is a fact the other person has to answer in the next. Nothing here is
# hidden from the reader and everything is hidden from the characters, which is the opposite of how the other
# nine work.
#
# The register is technical and affectionate, and it is the third story with nothing supernatural in it at all.
# A projectionist, two reels, and the year the cinema stopped.
#
# Ten scenes, three endings. No variables: the routing carries it.

title: Changeover
author: Roxanne
start: letter_one

== letter_one ==
bg booth_desk
music fan_hum
sfx projector
sfx projector
The carbon arc is the part nobody warns you about.

You are forty minutes into a two-hour film and the rod is down to the length of your thumb, and if you let it go
past that the light goes orange and then it goes out, and two hundred people in the dark find out that the film
they are watching was being held up by a person. You learn to hear it. It is not a sound exactly. It is the
projector sounding slightly more pleased with itself.

I am writing this on the back of a reel label because it is what is in front of me.

* Tell her about the arc, because she will laugh. -> letter_two_warm
* Tell her the cinema is closing. -> letter_two_plain

== letter_two_warm ==
bg booth_desk
She writes back in pencil, on the back of a shopping list, which is what she always did.

That is exactly it. That is the sound. Nobody believes me when I say you can hear a carbon arc going, and you have
described it in one line - it sounds pleased with itself - and I have been trying to say that since 1994.

I am in a flat with a window that faces a car park and I have not run a projector in eleven months and I still
look at the clock at twenty past the hour.

* Tell her you have been offered the job of running it down. -> letter_three

== letter_two_plain ==
bg booth_desk
She writes back in pencil, on the back of a shopping list, which is what she always did.

So it is happening. I have been waiting for that letter for two years and I still was not ready for it, which is
a stupid thing to be.

Eleven months since I ran anything. I still look at the clock at twenty past the hour. I do not know what I am
supposed to do with that.

* Tell her you have been offered the job of running it down. -> letter_three

== letter_three ==
bg booth_desk
They have offered me the run-down.

Six weeks, one screen, whatever we can still sell tickets for, and then the building goes to whoever is buying it.
I said I would think about it and I have been thinking about it for four days, which is four days longer than it
took me to say yes to the job in the first place.

* Take it. Somebody has to be the last one. -> letter_four_take
* Turn it down. You should not have to be the one who turns the lights off. -> letter_four_refuse

== letter_four_take ==
bg booth_desk
I took it.

I want to be careful about why, because the reason I gave them is not the reason. The reason I gave them is that I
know the building and the equipment and it is sensible. The reason is that I have been the one in the booth for
nineteen years and I am not going to hand the last six weeks to somebody who has never threaded a platter.

* Tell her that. -> letter_five

== letter_four_refuse ==
bg booth_desk
I turned it down.

They have given it to a man called Derek who I have never met and who, I am told, is very good with people. He
will run it down for six weeks and he will do it perfectly well and it will not matter to him at all, which is
probably the correct way to do it.

I have not slept properly since I said no, which tells you what I actually think.

* Tell her that. -> letter_five

== letter_five ==
bg booth_desk
She does not answer for eleven days, and then the letter is four pages long and the first page is about her
sister.

The second page is the one that matters. She says: I have been trying to work out whether I left because it was
ending or whether it ended because I left, and I have decided that I am never going to know, and I have decided
that I am allowed to stop asking.

* Write back that she is allowed. -> letter_six_allowed
* Write back that you do not think she is. -> letter_six_not

== letter_six_allowed ==
bg booth_desk
I wrote back and told her she was allowed, and that I had been asking the same question from the other side of it.

She will read that on a Thursday and I will not know what she thinks of it until the following Tuesday, which is
how this has always worked and is, I have decided, not a fault in it.

* And then the last week. -> last_week

== letter_six_not ==
bg booth_desk
I wrote back and told her I did not think she was allowed, because the place did not end when she left. It ended
when the last of us stopped.

That is a cruel thing to put in a letter and I knew it when I wrote it and I posted it anyway, and I have thought
about nothing else for six days.

* And then the last week. -> last_week

== last_week ==
bg booth_desk
The last week is six days and I run four of them.

On the Thursday there are nine people in a two-hundred-and-forty seat screen and I run the film properly anyway,
because that is the job, and because the alternative is deciding that nine people do not deserve a carbon arc
held at the right length.

On the Saturday I run the last one. I do the changeover by hand - there is no need, it is a platter, it has been a
platter since 1998 - and I do it by hand anyway, two reels, the way she taught me, and the splice holds.

* Write to her about the changeover. -> end_changeover
* Write to her about the nine people. -> end_nine
* Do not write. -> end_nothing

== end_changeover ==
bg booth_desk
I wrote to her about the changeover.

Two reels, the way she taught me, and the splice held - and she will know exactly what that means, which is the
only reason to write it down. Nobody else in the world would read that sentence and understand that I am telling
her it went well.

She writes back in pencil, on the back of a shopping list, and the whole letter is one line: I knew it would.

-> END

== end_nine ==
bg booth_desk
I wrote to her about the nine people.

Because that is the part I cannot get past. Nine people on a Thursday, and I held the arc at the right length for
all of them, and none of them will ever know that anyone did that.

She writes back in pencil, on the back of a shopping list: that is the job. that was always the job. you did it
for nineteen years and nine of them were the ones who found out.

-> END

== end_nothing ==
bg booth_desk
I did not write.

There is no reason for it. I had the letter in my pocket for four days and then the building was sold and then it
was a month later and then writing would have been a thing about the delay rather than a thing about the
changeover.

She writes anyway, in pencil, on the back of a shopping list, and she has heard, because people hear. The whole
letter is one line: I knew it would.

-> END
